  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <div class="section__header">
        <h2 class="section__title">How to Reduce Manufacturing Costs Without Cutting Quality</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section__text">
        <p>Reducing manufacturing costs doesn't require sacrificing quality. Strategic decisions about order quantity, design simplification, and supplier relationships can deliver 15-30% cost savings while maintaining or even improving product quality. The key is understanding which cost levers to pull and which trade-offs make sense for your brand positioning.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Increase MOQ gradually:</strong> Scaling from 300 to 500 pieces typically reduces per-unit cost by 10-15%. Moving from 500 to 1,000 pieces delivers another 10-15% savings. However, don't jump to high MOQs without sales confidence. Gradually increase quantities as you validate demand, using cost savings to fund growth rather than risking inventory on unproven designs.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Consolidate styles:</strong> Using fewer fabric types across your collection increases your purchasing power and reduces fabric minimums. A brand using 3 fabric types across 10 styles gets better pricing than a brand using 10 different fabrics. Consolidation also simplifies production planning and reduces error potential.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Simplify embellishments:</strong> Screen printing costs $0.50-2 per placement versus $1-5 for embroidery. Consider whether complex embellishments drive sufficient perceived value to justify the cost. Many successful brands use minimal, strategic embellishments that create impact without excessive cost.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Order seasonally, not reactively:</strong> Planning production 3-4 months ahead avoids 15-25% rush premiums. Reactive ordering when you're out of stock is expensive. Build a production calendar aligned with sales forecasts to secure standard pricing and reliable delivery timelines.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Build long-term factory relationships:</strong> Manufacturers reward loyalty with better pricing, priority scheduling, and more flexibility on problem resolution. A factory that knows your standards and trusts your payment reliability becomes a partner invested in your success, often passing efficiency gains back to you as cost savings.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Use full package production:</strong> Full package (FP) manufacturers handle fabric sourcing, production, and finishing as one service. This is typically more cost-effective than CMT (Cut, Make, Trim) where you source fabric separately and pay only for labor. FP manufacturers have fabric purchasing power you lack as an individual brand.</p>
        
        <p>Learn more about <a href="/full-package-production">full package production</a> and how it can reduce your total costs while simplifying your supply chain. The right production model depends on your volume, expertise, and strategic priorities.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <div class="section__header">
        <h2 class="section__title">Clothing Manufacturing Cost FAQ</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the average cost to manufacture a t-shirt?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Average t-shirt manufacturing cost ranges from $3-8 FOB for 300 pieces, $2-6 for 500 pieces, and $1-5 for 1000 pieces. Basic cotton t-shirts fall at the lower end, while premium organic cotton or custom prints increase cost. Bangladesh offers competitive pricing with 300-piece minimums.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How does MOQ affect manufacturing cost per unit?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Higher MOQ reduces cost per unit through production efficiency and fabric purchasing power. 1000-piece orders typically cost 30-50% less per unit than 300-piece orders. However, higher MOQ increases inventory risk. Brands must balance per-unit savings against cash flow and storage costs.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the difference between FOB and landed cost?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">FOB (Free On Board) is the factory price including production and delivery to the port. Landed cost includes FOB plus freight, insurance, customs duty, and any other charges to get goods to your warehouse. Landed cost is typically 20-40% higher than FOB depending on destination and shipping method.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What are standard payment terms for clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Standard payment terms are 30% deposit upon order confirmation, 70% balance before shipment. Letter of Credit offers security for larger orders. Western Union or PayPal may be accepted for small orders but carry higher fees. Always verify payment security before transferring funds.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How long does clothing manufacturing take from order to delivery?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Production timeline typically 30-45 days from fabric receipt. Sampling 7-14 days before bulk. Ocean freight to US/Europe 25-35 days. Air freight 5-7 days but significantly more expensive. Total order-to-delivery timeline 60-90 days for ocean freight, 40-55 days for air freight.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What hidden costs should I budget for in clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Hidden costs include sample fees ($50-200 per style), shipping samples, tech pack development ($200-1000), quality inspection ($200-500), bank fees for international transfers, customs broker fees, warehousing costs, and potential duty on imports. Budget 10-15% above quoted FOB for these items.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Which country offers the lowest clothing manufacturing costs?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Bangladesh offers the lowest costs for basic garments (t-shirts, hoodies, pants) with 300-1000 piece MOQs. Vietnam offers competitive pricing for technical garments. China offers speed and flexibility but at higher costs. Bangladesh combines low cost, duty advantages (GSP for EU), and established textile infrastructure.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I get an accurate manufacturing quote?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Provide detailed tech pack with measurements, fabric specifications, quantities per size/color, and delivery timeline. Specify any special requirements (certifications, labels, packaging). Request quotes from multiple manufacturers and compare not just price but communication, sample quality, and terms. Always request samples before bulk.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What certifications increase manufacturing costs?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">GOTS organic certification adds 15-25% to fabric costs. OEKO-TEX Standard 100 adds 5-10%. BSCI social compliance adds minimal cost but required for many retailers. ISO 9001 quality certification indicates process control. Certifications enable access to premium markets and justify higher retail pricing.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How does fabric choice affect manufacturing cost?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Fabric is typically 50-70% of garment cost. Basic cotton polyester blends are most cost-effective. Organic cotton costs 20-30% more. Recycled polyester adds 15-20%. Technical fabrics (Gore-Tex, specialized performance) can double costs. Fabric selection is the primary lever for controlling manufacturing costs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

